.TH CU 1C
.SH NAME
cu \- call UNIX
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cu
telno [
.B \-t
] [
.B \-s
speed ] [
.B \-a
acu ] [
.B \-l
line ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cu
calls up another UNIX or VENIX system, a terminal, or possibly a
non-UNIX system.
It manages an interactive conversation with possible
transfers of text files (transfer of binary files is not possible).
.I telno
is the
telephone number, with minus signs at appropriate places for delays.
(If
you do not have an automatic dialer,
.I telno
should be a dummy number.)
The
.B \-t
flag is used to dial out to a terminal.
.I speed
gives the transmission
speed (110, 150, 300, 1200, 2400, 4800, 9600); 300 is the default
value.
.PP
The
.B \-a
and
.B \-l
values may be used to specify pathnames for the ACU
and communications line devices.
They can be used to override the following
built-in defaults:
.PP
.nf
\fB\-a\fR /dev/null
\fB\-l\fR /dev/com1.dout
.fi
.PP
If no automatic call-up port is used, then
.I telno
can be any dummy
number, and the default
.B "\-a /dev/null"
should be used.
For ease of use,
.BR mknod (1)
can be used to make up a /dev/com1.dout which points to
whatever terminal port is actually used.
You must have both read and
write permission on this line.
.PP
After making the connection,
.B cu
runs as two processes: the
.I send
process
reads the standard input and passes most of it to the remote system; the
.I receive
process reads from the remote system and passes most data to the
standard output.
In general use, the
.I send
and
.I receive
processes simply
allow you to converse with the remote system: the
.I send
process sends
over everything you type at the keyboard (without echoing); the
.I receive
process writes to your terminal everything the other system types back
(including any echoing of what you just typed).
It is possible to specify
diversions to allow the redirection of I/O, however, by beginning a line
with a `~' (this means that files being sent should not have lines beginning
with `~').
Be aware that the
.I send
and
.I receive
processes handle these
diversions separately:
.I send
diversions can only be generated directly from
your keyboard, and
.I receive
diversions can only be generated from the
other system.
.PP
The
.I send
process interprets the following:
.TP 1.6i
~.
.PD 0
.TP
~EOT
terminate the conversation.
.PD
.TP
~<file
send the contents of
.I file
to the remote system, as
though typed at the terminal.
.TP
~!
invoke an interactive shell on the local system.
.TP
~!cmd ...
run the command on the local system (via
.BR "sh \-c" ).
See
.BR sh (1).
.TP
~$cmd ...
run the command locally and send its output to the
remote system.
.TP
~%take from [to]
copy file `from' (on the remote system) to file `to' on
the local system.
If `to' is omitted, the `from' name
is used both places.
.TP
~%put from [to]
copy file `from' (on local system) to file `to' on
remote system.
If `to' is omitted, the `from' name is
used both places.
.TP
~~...
send the line `~...'.
.PP
The
.I receive
process handles output diversions of the following form:
.PP
.nf
~>[>][:]file
zero or more lines to be written to file
~>
.fi
.PP
In any case, output is diverted (or appended, if `>>' used) to the file.
If `:' is used, the diversion is
.IR silent ,
i.e., it is written only to the file.
If
`:' is omitted, output is written both to the file and to the standard output.
The trailing `~>' terminates the diversion.
.PP
The use of
.BR ~%put
requires
.B stty
and
.BR cat (1)
on the remote side.
It also
requires that the current erase and kill characters on the remote system
be identical to the current ones on the local system.
Backslashes are
inserted at appropriate places.
.PP
The use of
.B ~%take
requires the existence of
.B echo
and
.BR tee (1)
on the
remote system.
Also,
.B "stty tabs"
mode is required on the remote system if
tabs are to be copied without expansion.
.PP
The following shell script can be run on the remote system to send a list
of files to the local system:
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta .5i
for f
do
	echo "~>$f"
	cat $f
	echo "~>"
done
.fi
.RE
.PP
For every file specified, the script sends to your
.I receive
process the proper
diversion code, the file, and then a diversion terminator.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following might be done to log in to a remote system and exchange
some files.
The user logs in on the remote system as user ``phil''.
After
exchanging files, the user writes a letter to phil; presumably the real Phil
will read it when he logs in.
.PP
.RS
.nf
.ta 1.2i
cu 1 \-s 300	(modem is 300 baud; dummy telno given)
login phil	(now talking to remote machine)
ls	(list files in phil's home directory)
~%take prog1.c	(copy prog1.c from remote system to yours)
~%put junk.c	(copy junk.c from your system to remote)
mail phil	(send mail to phil)
Phil \-
	I copied prog1.c, and left you
	junk.c
			\-Fred
^D	(cntrl\-D to end mail)
login	(log out of remote machine)
~.	(exit from cu)
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B cu
can be used to talk with non-UNIX systems, so long as these systems
can be told to type the appropriate `~' escape sequences.
The `put' and
`take' commands will probably not work, since they do assume certain
UNIX commands for creating these sequences.
.PP
.B cu
handles XON/XOFF protocols for both transmitting and receiving.
.SH FILES
.nf
.ta 2.4i
/dev/com1.dout
/dev/null
/bin/sh
/bin/echo, /bin/tee, /bin/stty	commands sent to remote system
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ttys(4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit code is zero for normal exit, nonzero (various values) otherwise.
.SH BUGS
The syntax is unique.
.PP
Lines of text which begin with `~' will be interpreted as commands when
sent over; if this is a problem, filter your files to add an additional `~' to
the beginning of any such line.
.PP
When executing local commands, a new shell is run to handle each command.
This means that doing a
.B ~!cd
will have no value, since the current
directory is changed only for the duration of the new shell.
.PP
To do several local commands in a row, type
.B ~!sh
to start up a temporary
shell which will stay in effect until a ^D is typed.
.sp
